    ],
    "exampleHtml": "I <span class=\"vp-verb\">had</span> my hair <span class=\"vp-prep\">cut</span>. · She <span class=\"vp-verb\">got</span> her phone <span class=\"vp-prep\">fixed</span>. · We <span class=\"vp-verb\">are having</span> the kitchen <span class=\"vp-prep\">painted</span>.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>have + something + past participle</strong> means someone else does a job for you. You don't do it yourself. <em>I had my hair cut.</em> (A hairdresser cut it.) <em>We're having the windows cleaned.</em> (A cleaner is doing it.)",
        "tag": "have something done"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>get + something + past participle</strong> has the same meaning. It is a little more informal. <em>She got her phone fixed. I need to get my car washed.</em>",
        "tag": "get something done"
      },
      {
        "html": "Only <strong>have</strong> or <strong>get</strong> changes with the tense. The past participle stays the same. <em>I have my hair cut every month. She's having her car repaired. We had the kitchen painted.</em> Questions and negatives use <em>do</em>: <em>Where did you have your hair cut? I didn't have it cut.</em>",
        "tag": "tenses"
      },
      {
        "html": "Sometimes the pattern is about bad luck. Then you did not ask for it: <em>He had his bike stolen. We had our car broken into.</em>",
        "tag": "bad luck"
      },
      {
        "html": "Typical mistakes: ✗ <em>I cut my hair at the salon.</em> → ✓ <em>I had my hair cut at the salon.</em> (a hairdresser did it) · ✗ <em>I had my hair to cut.</em> / <em>I had my hair cutting.</em> → ✓ <em>I had my hair cut.</em> · ✗ <em>Where did you had your hair cut?</em> → ✓ <em>Where did you have your hair cut?</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "The person who does the job is often not named. You can add <em>by</em> if you want to: <em>We had the roof repaired by a friend.</em> The past participle works like the verb in a passive sentence: <em>My hair was cut</em> means the same as <em>I had my hair cut</em>, but the passive does not say who asked for the job. See <a href=\"/passive-basics\">Passive basics</a>. For the forms of the past participle, see <a href=\"/present-perfect\">Present perfect or past simple</a>. For <em>make, let</em> and <em>help</em> with someone, see <a href=\"/make-let-help\">Make, let and help</a>. Don't use this pattern when you do the job yourself: <em>I painted the kitchen</em> (I did it), but <em>I had the kitchen painted</em> (a painter did it).",
    "reference": {
